Predicting this onto a grid of 60,000 points takes a couple hours, but as best I can tell the model runs to completion. I've tried alpha values from 1.1 to 2.0 and there is no visible difference.

Here are some basic parameters and details:

The study area is about 70 x 90 km. CRS is in meters, hence meters in the mesh parameters. I can cut the max.edge down 10-fold and see how it looks.

> bnd<-inla.nonconvex.hull(coords, convex=6000)

> cmv.mesh <- inla.mesh.2d(
+   boundary=bnd,
+   max.edge=c(3000, 9000),
+   cutoff=100)

> cmv.spde<-inla.spde2.pcmatern(mesh=cmv.mesh,
+                               prior.range = c(0.3,0.4),
+                               prior.sigma = c(100,0.5),
+                               alpha=2.0)

On Sun, Jan 22, 2017 at 9:06 PM, Finn Lindgren <finn.l...@gmail.com> wrote:
> Further:
> apart from computational issues that might prevent the calculations from completing (out of memory etc), if max.edge goes to zero, the alpha=2 model should in theory be virtually indistinguishable from a thin plate spline GAM, unless the priors contradict the data.
>
> Finn
