...and here's a way to check what constraints were imposed, or not, for each model:

> rbind(
+   c(mean(r7$summary.random$idx$mean),
+     mean(r8$summary.random$idx$mean),
+     mean(r9$summary.random$idx$mean)),
+   c(mean(r7$summary.random$idx$mean * (idx-mean(x))),
+     mean(r8$summary.random$idx$mean * (idx-mean(x))),
+     mean(r9$summary.random$idx$mean * (idx-mean(x)))))
              [,1]         [,2]       [,3]
[1,] -3.170377e-07 1.963278e-09  0.8923268
[2,]  5.520916e+01 5.178285e-01 70.8321573

Model 7&8 have sum-to-zero constraint. No other constraints are applied in any of the models.
The reason models 8 and 9 don't break down over the overparameterisation of slope is partly due to the prior imposed on all fixed effects. It's wide, but it's there. You can however see the effect on the very wide posterior prediction intervals for the slope parameter (as well as the rw2 values themselves); the strong negative correlation between the effects cancels this out, so that the combined predictor is still the same for all three models. You can check this by including the prediction intervals in the plot:

plot(idx,y)
lines(idx, r7$summary.linear.predictor$mean,col=2)
lines(idx, r8$summary.linear.predictor$mean,col=4)
lines(idx, r9$summary.linear.predictor$mean,col=5)

lines(idx, r7$summary.linear.predictor$mean+r7$summary.linear.predictor$sd,lty=2,col=2)
lines(idx, r7$summary.linear.predictor$mean-r7$summary.linear.predictor$sd,lty=2,col=2)
lines(idx, r8$summary.linear.predictor$mean+r8$summary.linear.predictor$sd,lty=2,col=4)
lines(idx, r8$summary.linear.predictor$mean-r8$summary.linear.predictor$sd,lty=2,col=4)
lines(idx, r9$summary.linear.predictor$mean+r9$summary.linear.predictor$sd,lty=2,col=5)
lines(idx, r9$summary.linear.predictor$mean-r9$summary.linear.predictor$sd,lty=2,col=5)

Finn

